# AGENTS.md — {{.Name}}

The Go service next to the Rails app. Generated by Bogie (layout
{{.LayoutVersion}}, recorded in `bogie.toml`). Bogie is not a dependency:
nothing here imports it, and the code is yours.

**The layout is Rails-shaped on purpose.** Do not "fix" it into idiomatic Go
layout. Underscored package names such as `admin_controller`, one per Rails
namespace, are deliberate; `.golangci.yml` disables ST1003 for exactly this
and nothing else.

## Commands

    make help           every task
    make up             postgres on :5440 (docker compose), healthy before it returns
    make db-prepare     create + migrate + seed, like rails db:prepare
    make db-seed        apply db/seeds through the binary; g_seed_<name> authors one
    make credentials    edit the encrypted settings; ENV=production for that file
    make db-migrate     apply pending migrations; db-rollback, db-migrate-status, db-reset as in Rails
    make g_migration_<name>   author a migration (go tool goose)
    make server         run the HTTP server (go run . serve); migrates at boot
    bogie server        the same, with live reload in development (Air)
    make test           go test -race -cover ./...
    make lint           gofmt check, go vet, golangci-lint
    bin/ci              every check, run locally; green ends with gh signoff

`bogie` runs the same commands from any subdirectory, spelled as Rails spells
them: `bogie db:prepare`, `db:migrate`, `db:rollback`, `db:seed`,
`credentials:edit -e production`, `server` (`s`), `test` (`t`). The tool adds the Rails
spelling and nothing else; the app never imports it. In development
`bogie server` and `bogie worker` run under Air, so a saved file rebuilds
and restarts the process; `.air.toml` holds the settings and deleting it
turns reload off. `make server` never reloads, which is the thing to run
when you want one boot and one set of logs. A newer Bogie moves the
app to its layout with `bogie app:update`, a three-way merge of the files the
templates produce against the version `bogie.toml` records; your edits and
everything the generators wrote are kept, and a real overlap leaves conflict
markers.

CI runs here, not on a hosted runner: `bin/ci` is the whole pipeline (Rails
8.1's bin/ci, ported), and a green run on a clean tree records a signoff
status on the commit. Run it before you push. Add a new check as one line at
the bottom of `bin/ci`.

## Configuration

Encrypted credentials, the Rails pattern, via `bogie-go/credentials`:
committed ciphertext, keys never committed, environment variables override.

    config/credentials.yml.enc            + config/master.key              development
    config/credentials/staging.yml.enc    + config/credentials/staging.key
    config/credentials/production.yml.enc + config/credentials/production.key

`make credentials` edits development's; `make credentials ENV=production`
edits that file with its own key. Every file is flat and has one section,
`{{.Name}}:`, whose keys give the environment-variable names: `{{.Name}}.addr`
is overridden by `{{.EnvPrefix}}_ADDR`.

Two variables, deliberately separate: `{{.EnvPrefix}}_ENV` is a closed set,
`development`, `test`, `production`, and selects behaviour; staging is
`{{.EnvPrefix}}_ENV=production {{.EnvPrefix}}_CREDENTIALS=staging`, production
strictness with staging secrets. In a container the key arrives as
`{{.EnvPrefix}}_MASTER_KEY` and no key file exists. **Test never reads a
credentials file**: it runs from defaults and the environment, so tests need
nobody's secrets and can never open the development database.

## Layout

    main.go                      dispatches on the first argument: serve |{{if .Jobs}} worker |{{end}} migrate | db
    cli.go                       the migrate and db subcommands
    app/
      application.go             wiring: migrate at boot, config, connections, controllers
      controllers/
        application.go           root Server, one NAMED field per controller
        routes.go                the whole routing table
        welcome.go               the development page at /; gone once a route claims /
        <name>_controller.go     one Rails controller: a <Name>Controller, its routes, its actions
        <ns>_controller/         one Rails namespace: server.go · routes.go · <name>_controller.go …
      middlewares/               request id · logging · recover{{if .Jobs}}
      jobs/                      River workers, one file per job, registered above // bogie:jobs{{end}}
      services/<name>/           ctx first, knows nothing about HTTP
      services/database/         db create and drop, through the maintenance database
      services/migrate/          goose over the embedded migrations and seeds
      models/                    sqlc output plus the store
      views/                     wire contracts; domain structs never double as wire structs
      domain/                    THE DOMAIN; imports nothing third-party
    config/                      credentials file + env → validated struct, no globals
      deploy.yml                 Kamal 2, staging; deploy.production.yml overlays it
    .kamal/secrets               what Kamal substitutes: the master key and the registry password
    Dockerfile                   one image, every role; keys never enter it (.dockerignore)
    db/
      db.go                      go:embed of migrate/*.sql and seeds/*.sql
      migrate/                   goose SQL, timestamped; `bogie g model` writes the first
      seeds/                     goose SQL in its own version table; development data
    lib/logger/

Tests live beside the code they test, stdlib `testing` only.

## Deploy

Kamal 2, as the Rails app next door. `config/deploy.yml` IS staging and a
bare `kamal deploy` targets it; `kamal deploy -d production` overlays
`config/deploy.production.yml`. Three things to fill in first: the servers,
the host names and the registry, marked CHANGE-ME. The image is one binary
in every role{{if .Jobs}}, and the `job` role is that image started as `{{.Name}} worker`{{end}};
it migrates at boot, so the database only has to exist. The encrypted
credentials travel in the image and the key arrives as `{{.EnvPrefix}}_MASTER_KEY`
from `.kamal/secrets`, which reads `config/credentials/staging.key`.
`make credentials ENV=staging` makes that file and key the first time.

## The six rules

1. `ctx context.Context` is the first parameter of anything doing I/O. No
   `context.TODO()`.
2. Interfaces are declared where they are CONSUMED, next to the code that uses
   them, holding only the methods it uses. Never exported from the package
   that implements them.
3. `app/domain` imports nothing third-party.
4. Constructors return `(T, error)`. Only `main` exits.
5. `*gin.Context` never travels below `app/controllers`. A handler converts
   HTTP into domain values, calls one function, and converts the result back.
6. No `util/`, `helpers/` or `common/`. The package name is a required prefix
   at every call site, so it has to say what it provides.

## Recipes

### Add a resource

    bogie g scaffold post title:string body:text

is `rails g scaffold`: the model (everything `g model` writes, below), the
wire shapes in `app/views/posts.go`, and `app/controllers/posts_controller.go`
with the five resourceful actions over the store, registered with the store
passed in. There is no service layer: the controller declares the slice of
the store it uses as an interface beside it (rule 2), and the store runs the
model's `Validate` on create and update, as Rails validates on save, so a bad
value is a 422 from the store. The model is named in the singular and the
views and controller in the plural, as Rails names them. `bogie d scaffold
post` removes all of it. When a resource outgrows CRUD, `bogie g service`
gives the logic a home that knows nothing about HTTP.

### Add a controller

    bogie g controller comments index show create update destroy

One Rails controller is one Go file, `app/controllers/comments_controller.go`
in the `controllers` package: a `CommentsController` type holding its
dependencies, `SetupRoutes`, and one method per action, with a test beside
it. Name a resource in the plural, as Rails does: the name is used as typed,
so `comments` answers at `/comments` and `comment` at `/comment`. Go has no
autoload, so a controller exists only once it is constructed,
held and mounted. That is three one-line insertions, each above a marker
comment, and the generator makes all three, idempotently:

1. `app/controllers/application.go`, above `// bogie:controllers`: a named
   field, `Comments *CommentsController`.
2. `app/application.go`, above `// bogie:wire`: its construction,
   `server.Comments = controllers.NewCommentsController(log)`.
3. `app/controllers/routes.go`, above `// bogie:routes`: its mount,
   `s.Comments.SetupRoutes(&r.RouterGroup)`.

The actions are stubs answering 501 until you fill them in; index, show,
create, update and destroy get their resourceful routes, any other name is a
GET under that name, and new and edit are refused because nothing here renders
a form. Give the controller its dependencies as a small interface declared
beside it and pass the real thing on the wire line. `bogie d controller
comments` removes all of it, registration included. `bogie doctor` checks the
markers are intact and every controller is constructed and mounted. Never
delete a marker: the generators stop and name the file if one is missing;
they do not guess.

### Give a controller a dependency

A generated controller holds only a logger. To hand it a store or a service,
three edits, all in files you own:

1. Beside the controller, declare the slice it needs (rule 2), only the
   methods it calls:

        type commentsStore interface {
            GetComment(ctx context.Context, id string) (domain.Comment, error)
        }

2. Add a field and a constructor parameter: `Store commentsStore`,
   `NewCommentsController(store commentsStore, log *slog.Logger)`.
3. On its wire line in `app/application.go`, pass the real thing:
   `server.Comments = controllers.NewCommentsController(store, log)`.
   `*models.Store` satisfies any interface made of its own methods.

The test hands in a fake with the same methods; it needs no database. A
scaffolded controller shows the finished shape.

### Add a namespace

    bogie g controller admin/reports index show

A Rails namespace (`namespace :admin`, `Admin::ReportsController`) is a Go
package: `app/controllers/admin_controller/`, with `server.go` holding one
named field per controller above its own `// bogie:controllers`, and
`routes.go` mounting the `/admin` group above its own `// bogie:routes`.
Middleware every controller in the namespace shares, an auth check or a
shared secret, goes on that group, so the exposure boundary is visible in one
place. The first `g controller admin/...` writes the package and registers it
in the root with the same three lines a controller gets; each controller in
it is then `reports_controller.go` beside `server.go`, held by the namespace's
`Server`, mounted in the namespace's `routes.go`, and still constructed in
`app/application.go`: `server.Admin.Reports = admin_controller.NewReportsController(log)`.
Dependencies never thread through the namespace. One level of namespace; a
version such as `v1` is a route group inside the namespace's `routes.go`.
`bogie d controller admin/reports` removes the controller, and the package
too once it holds no controller.

{{if .Jobs}}### Add a background job

    bogie g job send_welcome

writes `app/jobs/send_welcome.go`, an Args type with a `Kind` and a Worker with
`Work(ctx, job)`, and registers it above `// bogie:jobs` in `app/jobs/jobs.go`.
Jobs run on River, on the same Postgres: enqueue with
`app.Jobs.Insert(ctx, jobs.SendWelcomeArgs{...}, nil)`, or inside a
transaction with `InsertTx`, so the job commits with the row or not at all.
`bogie worker` (or `make worker`) works the queue; it is its own process so a
backlog never slows a request. Args are stored as JSON: carry ids, not
records. River's schema is applied by `migrate`, not by db/migrate.

{{end}}### Add authentication

    bogie g authentication secret      # the Rails app calls this service
    bogie g authentication token       # a user presents a bearer token
    bogie g authentication api_key     # a machine client presents a key

Each shape is a middleware on a route group, as `before_action` is. The
generator writes the middleware and its test in `app/middlewares`, a group
helper on the root Server in `app/controllers/authentication_<shape>.go`,
and the config: a field, its environment override and a production check,
above the markers in `config/config.go`, with a development value written
into the credentials file so the app boots as before. Staging and
production get theirs with `make credentials ENV=...`, and refuse to boot
without. Then mount what the guard protects on the group instead of the
router, in `routes.go`:

    s.Posts.SetupRoutes(s.WithSecret(&r.RouterGroup))   // or WithUser, WithAPIKey

- **secret**: the `X-<App>-Secret` header (`middlewares.SecretHeader`),
  constant-time compared, Basic auth as a fallback for a browser. The Rails
  side is one header on its client, from its own credentials.
- **token**: JWT HS256 with `{{.Name}}.token_secret`. The Rails app mints a
  token for its user with the `jwt` gem and this service verifies it;
  `middlewares.Subject(c)` is the subject in a handler. A service that signs
  users in itself mints with `s.Tokens().Mint(subject, ttl)`.
- **api_key**: an `api_keys` table with a bcrypt digest, a service, and
  `bogie api_keys:create NAME`, which prints the key once;
  `middlewares.APIKeyName(c)` says which key a request presented.

`bogie d authentication <shape>` removes it; the development value stays in
the credentials file, harmless.

### Add a service

    bogie g service publish_post

writes `app/services/publish_post/` with a `Service`, a `store` interface to
fill with only the methods it calls, a `Run(ctx)` to rename, and a test. It is
not wired anywhere: the controller that needs it declares what it wants. A
service is for logic that is not CRUD; a plain resource needs none.

### Add a model

    bogie g model comment body:text post:references score:integer

writes the `create_comments` migration, `db/queries/comments.sql` with get,
list, create, update and delete, `app/domain/comment.go` with the type and an
empty `Validate`, and `app/models/comments.go`, the store methods that convert
sqlc's row type to `domain.Comment`, run `Validate` before create and update,
and map no-rows to `domain.ErrNotFound`; then it runs sqlc. Types: string, text, integer, bigint, boolean, datetime,
uuid, jsonb, references; `:index` or `:uniq` after a type. `post:references`
is the column `post_id` with a foreign key and an index.

Every generated column is NOT NULL with a type-appropriate default, so sqlc
emits plain Go types rather than pointers. A column that truly needs null is
one edit to the migration.

### Change a table

    bogie g migration add_slug_to_posts slug:string:uniq
    bogie g migration remove_draft_from_posts draft:boolean

`create_<table>`, `add_<x>_to_<table>` and `remove_<x>_from_<table>` get their
SQL written; any other name gets an empty Up and Down to fill in. The
migrations are what sqlc compiles against, so the generator runs sqlc
immediately and `app/models/*.sql.go` changes before the migration is ever
applied. `bogie db:migrate` applies it; `bogie db:rollback` checks the Down.
Queries go in `db/queries/<table>.sql`; `make sqlc` recompiles them, and a
wrong column name fails there, not in production. The generated files are
committed; `bin/ci` fails if they are stale.

The schema is owned here and applied at boot; there is no schema dump. A new
app has no table, as `rails new` has none: `bogie g model` writes the first,
and `bin/ci` runs the database steps against whatever the migrations hold.

### Add a query

There is no `where` builder; a query is SQL with a name. In
`db/queries/comments.sql`:

    -- name: ListCommentsForPost :many
    SELECT * FROM comments WHERE post_id = $1 ORDER BY created_at DESC;

then `make sqlc`, which writes `ListCommentsForPost` into
`app/models/comments.sql.go` with typed parameters, or fails if a column is
misspelled. Then a store method in `app/models/comments.go` that calls it
and converts the rows with `commentFromRow`, as the generated methods do.
Annotations: `:one`, `:many`, `:exec`, `:execrows`. The generated file is
committed; `bin/ci` fails if it is stale.

### Write a test

Tests sit beside the code, stdlib `testing`, no factories and no fixtures.

- A **controller test** mounts the controller alone on `gin.New()` with a
  fake of its interface and asserts on status and body. No database; the
  scaffolded `*_controller_test.go` is the pattern.
- A **store test** calls `testStore(t)`, which opens the TEST database,
  migrates it and truncates every table, so each test starts from nothing.
  It needs Postgres up (`make up`) and never touches development data.
- A **domain test** is a plain function call: `Validate` and whatever rules
  live in `app/domain`, nothing to set up.
- A **service test** fakes the store interface the service declared.

`bogie test` runs everything with the race detector and coverage.

### When something is off

- `bogie doctor`: every marker exactly once, every controller constructed
  and mounted, tools pinned, layout files present.
- `make sqlc` after any change to `db/queries` or `db/migrate`; a stale
  `*.sql.go` fails `bin/ci`.
- A migration that will not roll back: `bogie db:rollback` runs the Down
  block, so write one.
- A 500 in a handler is logged with `request_id`; the response says
  "internal error" and nothing else, on purpose.
